> Actually, the rtems-init.c file is the configuration file for the RTOS and
this header will
appear during the boot-up.
Here's how fio is interfaced with RTEMS:
- After cross-compiling the fio sources with RTEMS toolchain, user will
have to directly
  load the generated fio binary using an appropriate bootloader(like
u-boot) on the target(like on beaglebone black).
- Then, RTEMS will bootup and the application specific header will
appear(the one i have put above).
- RTEMS shell is then invoked (rtems_shell_init()), and user can enter the
job config file and then invoke fio.
- In any case, rtems_shell_init() should not exit, and if it does we have
assert(0) which will immediately stop the kernel.


